# Captured data NEVER leaves this machine.
#
# data/ holds harvested session state: the SQLite store, raw status line payloads, and
# pre-compaction transcript SNAPSHOTS, which are verbatim copies of real conversations and
# carry session ids, account and organization UUIDs, working directory paths and file contents.
# It also holds a backup of the user's settings.json. None of it is publishable, and a private
# repository is not a good enough reason to put it in git history where it cannot be removed.
data/

# Scratch. Self-ignoring by convention in this project.
tmp/

# Per-project harness state (hook exec-state files), not source.
# The launch config IS source: it is how the app gets started.
.claude/*
!.claude/launch.json
# And anywhere else a tool drops one. A hook wrote its per-session state into
# frontend/.claude/hooks/ and it was staged before anyone noticed; the rule above only
# covered the repository root.
**/.claude/

# Python bytecode
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# OS and editor noise
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Session records: the plan/result/review/verify trail this extension was built from.
#
# These are working documents, not repository content. They carry the machine they were written
# on - home directory, drive letters, unrelated project names, session uuids - and they describe
# process rather than the extension. A reader who clones this repo wants the extension.
#
# They stay ON DISK deliberately. They are the only account of WHY the code is shaped this way:
# why status line samples carry a probe flag, why windows are resolved per model segment, why a
# minified identifier may never anchor a claim. Read them before changing behaviour they explain.
.md/

# Notes and proposals dropped in for review. Some are working documents about OTHER tools, carrying
# third-party product descriptions verbatim; others are proposals about THIS extension that were
# written against a snapshot of it and are already wrong in specifics. Neither kind is source, and a
# stale proposal in the repo reads like a decision. They stay on disk, out of git.
Extension Features and Context Capture.md
Claude Code Context Capture Extension - Analytical UX Report.md
Claude-Code-Context-Capture Technical Review.md
React+TypeScript Dashboard.md

# The React frontend's dependencies: 149 MB, restorable from the tracked package-lock.json.
frontend/node_modules/

# frontend/dist IS tracked, which is unusual for build output and is the point.
#
# The UI is now the only one, and the README's headline is that install is three commands and pulls
# NOTHING from npm. If running the dashboard required `npm install` first, that would stop being
# true: a reader who just wants to look at their own context window would have to fetch 149 MB of
# build tooling to do it. So the 4.2 MB build is committed and `python -m c4x.api` serves it.
#
# npm is needed to CHANGE the frontend, not to run it. The Plotly chunk is content-hashed and only
# rewrites when Plotly's version does, so ordinary edits add roughly 240 KB per build.

# Generated documentation. `tools/docgen/` is source and IS tracked; these are what it produces.
# 17 MB of PNG and JSON plus a 6.2 MB .docx, rebuildable from the source beside them, and a git
# history is a poor place for a 23 MB artifact that changes wholesale on every run.
docs/docgen/
docs/c4x-field-guide.docx
